<!-- @diagram 6b7c18c9f8073fff -->
### Who does what

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>ViewModel / UI</strong><small><code>enqueue</code></small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>WorkManager</strong><small>1 · saves state in its Room database · 2 · delegates to JobScheduler</small></div><div class="node"><strong>System scheduler</strong><small>3 · wakes the app when conditions hold</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong><code>CoroutineWorker</code></strong><small>4 · runs, then status is updated in the database</small></div></div><figcaption>Your code only enqueues and does the work; persistence and scheduling belong to WorkManager and the system.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Tradeoffs :: check -->
**Will it run?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>Periodic work requested every 5 minutes.</summary>

Not that often: periodic work has a minimum interval of 15 minutes. Use a different design for near-real-time updates.
</details>

<details><summary>A sync enqueued with <code>ExistingWorkPolicy.KEEP</code> while an identical one is already queued.</summary>

The new request is dropped and the queued one stays, so taps do not stack up duplicate syncs.
</details>

<details><summary>A Hilt worker whose dependencies are constructor-injected, with the default initializer still enabled.</summary>

WorkManager creates workers with its default factory, which cannot supply those dependencies, so creating the worker fails. Use `HiltWorkerFactory` and on-demand initialization.
</details>

